course: dictionary
lesson: 6270
title: Money
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Money is coins and bills (notes) that people use to buy things. It can also be the amount you have in a bank or on a card, even when you cannot hold it in your hand.
explain: Look at the words around money to know which meaning is right. «Money boxes with counters can be wrong, so count yourself now and then.» Here money means the coins and notes you have saved, and count means check the total yourself.
explain: Money can also mean how much something is worth or how rich a person is. Slang words such as «quid», «dosh» and «cash» also mean money, but in a formal exam you would write funds or a sum of money.
fact: Money is an uncountable noun, so you say some money or a lot of money, not a money or moneys.
fact: «Monies» is rare and formal, so do not use it in everyday writing.
fact: «Fixed costs» are bills that come every month, like rent, energy and council tax; they are paid before money goes on fun.
fact: «On the money» means exactly right, and «money's tight» means there is not much money.
fact: A charity is a group that helps people or animals; to donate is to give money to help.
exercise: Nadia says «quid» is slang. What does quid mean? | A sweet from the shop | *A pound, the money used in the UK | A bird that sings at night | A type of bag
exercise: «Money boxes with counters can be wrong, so count yourself now and then.» Find and copy the word that means check the total. | money | boxes | *count | wrong
exercise: «Fixed costs» are bills that come every month. Which of these is a fixed cost? | *Rent | Ice cream at the beach | A birthday present for a friend | Sweets on a Friday
test: What does «money's tight» mean? | Money is being given away for free | The coins are stuck together | There is a lot of money in the bank | *There is not much money
test: Which word is the most formal way to say money in an exam? | *Funds | Dosh | Quid | Loot
exam: In the phrase «total expenditure incurred», what does expenditure mean? | A type of bank card | The number of coins in a purse | *The amount of money spent | A long, slow walk
